00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.163
[Peter] How are you doing?

00:07:46.163 --> 00:07:48.070
-What's your name?
-Vince. Vincent Brown.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.110
Vince, yeah, Peter.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.110
-He's good people.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.176
-So Vince, you grew up out here.
-Yeah.

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:54.405
Small town sh*t, you know?

00:07:54.405 --> 00:07:55.405
-Small town sh*t?

00:07:55.405 --> 00:07:56.555
What about the animals?

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:58.592
-Bears and panthers.
-Bears.

00:07:58.592 --> 00:08:00.856
Well, we had a bear attack
a little while back.

00:08:00.856 --> 00:08:01.884
-An attack?

00:08:01.884 --> 00:08:05.095
Yeah, I got a bear in a guy's house
and took him out of it. An old man.

00:08:05.095 --> 00:08:07.245
-The 89 year old man.
-Do you remember that?

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.696
I don't know if the old man was feeding
the bear or not or what was going on.

00:08:10.696 --> 00:08:11.746
You know what I mean?

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.469
That's the worst thing
you can do is feed one.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.212
You know what I mean? Feed anything.

00:08:16.212 --> 00:08:18.362
If you don't want it around, don't feed it.

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:20.743
-[Brian] I'm going to have him bring one.
-[Peter] What?

00:08:20.743 --> 00:08:21.821
A python.

00:08:21.821 --> 00:08:23.871
[Peter] He's bringing a python? [giggles]

00:08:24.597 --> 00:08:26.322
I want to see a python.

00:08:27.915 --> 00:08:29.680
Did you know about this place, Harrison?

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:30.810
I knew about Copeland.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:34.010
I didn't know that we would be able to
meet so many friends here.

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:36.543
-You told me Totch was your uncle.
-Yeah.

00:08:36.543 --> 00:08:40.550
I had been… Totch's book is my favorite
book I've ever read, hands down.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:43.000
-I know that sounds crazy, but…
-[Peter] Who's Totch?

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.718
Totch was a Chokoloskee legend.

00:08:44.718 --> 00:08:47.691
He was born in 1920. He died in 1996.

00:08:47.691 --> 00:08:50.759
He was a fisherman.
He was a Medal of Honor recipient.

00:08:50.759 --> 00:08:53.817
-[Vince] Yeah. Smuggler.
-Smuggler. He lived off the land.

00:08:53.817 --> 00:08:57.856
I mean, a true polymath
of this corner of the world.

00:08:57.856 --> 00:08:59.551
-Gator hunter.
-Gator hunter. All of it.

00:08:59.551 --> 00:09:01.357
That's probably the biggest thing he did.

00:09:01.357 --> 00:09:03.612
I love the way his book
was written in black and white.

00:09:03.612 --> 00:09:05.576
Like, this happened,
this happened, this happened.

00:09:05.576 --> 00:09:07.371
So you know there was nothing
but just facts.

00:09:07.371 --> 00:09:08.058
Yeah.

00:09:08.058 --> 00:09:10.162
And the way he approached life
with this fearlessness

00:09:10.162 --> 00:09:13.640
and then this love for the openness,
just deeply resonates with me.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:17.640
Totch was one of the true pioneers
of the modern day out here.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.090
Yeah, he was definitely
a pioneer of everybody.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:21.510
Yeah, and his family too.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:23.709
So he got into the smuggling big time?

00:09:23.709 --> 00:09:25.609
Yeah. Yeah, he's got us…

00:09:25.609 --> 00:09:27.359
He got my dad into it.

00:09:28.336 --> 00:09:30.270
-Yeah.
-That was huge out here?

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:31.270
Yeah, in the '80s.

00:09:31.523 --> 00:09:33.240
In the late '70s and '80s.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.740
-'83 was a blockade, right?
-Yeah, smuggling marijuana on shrimp boats.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.153
[Peter] Where was
the marijuana coming from?

00:09:39.153 --> 00:09:41.114
Mostly Columbia, but Jamaica.

00:09:41.114 --> 00:09:42.656
Then coming right off the shore here?

00:09:42.662 --> 00:09:44.428
Yeah, it come right off shore here

00:09:44.428 --> 00:09:46.794
and then we'd get there
with, call 'em the go-fast boats.

00:09:47.517 --> 00:09:51.589
Like 24 foot Morgans
with two engines on them.

00:09:51.589 --> 00:09:54.010
Go out there and get them
and bring them up creeks and

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.263
put it on vans
and take it down the highway.

00:09:57.543 --> 00:10:01.550
I was like 14, 15 years old when all this
was going on. You know, I was just a kid.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.558
I started smoking pot.
It was just fun.

00:10:04.558 --> 00:10:06.734
We had all the money we wanted.
You know what I mean?

00:10:06.734 --> 00:10:08.530
We just had anything we wanted.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:11.580
We wanted a new motorcycle or something,
just went and got it.

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:12.777
It was just…

00:10:13.371 --> 00:10:17.171
And then we run out of money, we're all
sitting there like, oh, God. [laughs]

00:10:17.763 --> 00:10:22.110
-What's the replacement economy out here?
There's nothing really.

00:10:22.111 --> 00:10:23.742
Nothing. You go back fishing.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.950
[Peter] Okay, so they said
come to the backside of this house.

00:10:29.951 --> 00:10:31.901
Yeah, it looks like a compound in here.

00:10:34.036 --> 00:10:35.681
[Harrison] "Stay out of  my truck."

00:10:35.681 --> 00:10:37.896
"Ain't nothing that a beer can't fix."

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.376
Well guys, we're far out there.

00:10:42.376 --> 00:10:44.504
We're going to get back
to the nature though.

00:10:44.504 --> 00:10:45.269
-Yes.

00:10:45.269 --> 00:10:47.434
But look, this is an interesting

00:10:48.139 --> 00:10:50.424
part of the state, the country,

00:10:51.301 --> 00:10:52.301
the world.

00:10:52.519 --> 00:10:53.610
[Peter] Oh my God.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:55.565
They didn't want to come out.

00:10:55.565 --> 00:10:57.465
It's cold. I left the female in there.

00:10:58.017 --> 00:11:00.202
[Peter] Okay, so what do you do
with the snakes, Wayne?

00:11:00.202 --> 00:11:02.352
I've had these guys since they were babies.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:05.430
Okay, you just like…
they're pets for you.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:06.430
Oh yeah.

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:09.650
Come here. This one I named Stripe
'cause of the long stripe.

00:11:09.885 --> 00:11:11.135
This one here is a dwarf.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:14.870
I got him from the neighbor.
He wasn't taking care of it. Ten bucks.

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:16.750
I finally talked him out of it
for ten dollars.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.271
-Pythons?
-Yeah, these are ball pythons.

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:22.030
Ball pythons. How big do they get?

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:24.390
They only get up six foot.
This one's full grown.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.678
I got them in a big 150 gallon
aquarium with logs and everything else,

00:11:27.678 --> 00:11:29.400
and a waterfall and all that in there.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:30.690
It's not easy to get these.

00:11:30.691 --> 00:11:33.386
-Have you lived out here your whole life?
-I've been here 37 years.

00:11:33.386 --> 00:11:34.910
But I grew up out in the Everglades.

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:37.432
-You love it?
-I love it. I've been here all my life.

00:11:37.432 --> 00:11:39.653
I'm 69 years old right now.

00:11:39.653 --> 00:11:40.653
-Why do you love it?

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:43.864
It's because I'm away from
all the crazy people in town.

00:11:43.864 --> 00:11:46.064
I hate town. [laughs]

00:11:46.357 --> 00:11:47.650
[laughing] Here, put it on him.

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:50.610
I'm good, Wayne.
Just want a shot with me and you.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:51.925
-And snakes.
-There you go.

00:11:51.925 --> 00:11:52.875
-There we go.
-Here.

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:56.250
Come here, look. Let's get them over here.
I'm getting him closer.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.400
I'm getting him closer.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.440
-That's as close as he's gonna go.
-That's about as close…

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:01.407
-[laughter]

00:12:01.407 --> 00:12:03.054
I didn't start it with him.

00:12:04.257 --> 00:12:05.257
[country music]

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:15.050
[Harrison] We're on Tamiami Trail now,
which connects Miami to Tampa.

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:16.550
This was built in 1927.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:19.049
It was the first road
that came through out here.

00:12:19.049 --> 00:12:21.110
-It's like the Savannah
a little bit, right?

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:22.242
Right. No, for sure.

00:12:22.242 --> 00:12:25.322
I always tell people, if you took a picture
of that in the right light,

00:12:25.322 --> 00:12:28.855
and you told somebody you were in Africa,
they might just believe you.

00:12:28.855 --> 00:12:29.509
-Yeah.

00:12:29.509 --> 00:12:31.054
Just missing the lions, you know?

00:12:31.054 --> 00:12:32.339
Oh, a big alligator right there.

00:12:32.339 --> 00:12:34.320
-You want to go look?
-Oh, yeah. Let's do it.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.676
[Peter] There's one sunbathing.

00:12:36.965 --> 00:12:38.015
Cold night last night.

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:40.464
I think lower 40s here.

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:42.210
It's warming up.

00:12:42.815 --> 00:12:44.229
This guy getting some sun.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:47.975
And so I guess
they're all along these banks.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:50.370
-Pretty cool.
I just noticed actually, look.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.257
He's missing the back part of his tail.

00:12:52.257 --> 00:12:53.540
-Oh, yeah. Right.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:55.660
I don't know if
the camera's going to catch it, but…

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:57.670
Yeah, he lost a tail.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:12:59.270
-Must be a good war story.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.670
[Harrison] The smallest
post office in America, in Ochopee.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.470
[Peter] Okay, yeah, it is. For sure.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.480
Here we go, guys.
I've seen a lot of small ones

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:11.454
out in the desert west.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:15.490
Utah, Arizona, but this wins.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:16.657
Look at this place.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:18.310
It's like an outhouse.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:20.710
Ochopee Post Office.

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.940
"Considered to be the smallest post office
in the United States.

00:13:23.946 --> 00:13:26.800
This building was formerly
an irrigation pipe shed

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.600
belonging to the JT Guant
Company tomato farm."

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.450
Okay, so they did tomatoes down here.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.530
Hey, how you doing, sir?

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:35.650
Not bad. Not bad.

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.260
-That's cool.
-Smallest one around.

00:13:37.266 --> 00:13:38.995
You're a US postal worker.

00:13:38.995 --> 00:13:40.550
I really am.

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:42.114
Oh, that's cool, Donald.

00:13:42.617 --> 00:13:45.010
Can I poke the camera
to show what this office is like?

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.060
Check it out. It's a cool little spot
to check out. Heck yeah.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:50.790
Lot of little knick-knacks in here.

00:13:50.791 --> 00:13:51.791
-Oh, that's cool.

00:13:51.791 --> 00:13:54.264
People given me over the years
'cause it's such a tiny little thing.

00:13:54.264 --> 00:13:56.235
-How many people have a P.O. box here?

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:57.630
-Nobody.
-Nobody?

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:59.219
Nobody has a P.O. box here.

00:13:59.219 --> 00:14:01.541
It's all street delivery.
We have a carrier out of here.

00:14:01.541 --> 00:14:02.611
It's a big area, too.

00:14:02.611 --> 00:14:05.519
It's not just, like, right around here
where you see a couple buildings.

00:14:05.519 --> 00:14:07.864
I mean, we go, like,
50-something miles that way.

00:14:07.864 --> 00:14:08.603
-Okay.

00:14:08.603 --> 00:14:10.711
We go down to the light
and then out, like, 20 miles.

00:14:10.711 --> 00:14:14.029
So, it's like a 140-mile round trip
every day for the carrier.

00:14:14.029 --> 00:14:15.429
-Oh, that's cool.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:14:15.429 --> 00:14:18.390
It's a long gig.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.640
But it's pretty nice out here.
You can't beat…

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.030
-Hey, how are you?
-How are you?

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:26.030
Remind me your name?

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:27.210
My name is Lisa.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.036
Lisa, it's great to see you. Look at this
beautiful day we're having.

00:14:30.036 --> 00:14:32.115
-Lisa, what's the fun fact of the day?

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:34.508
Fun fact is, I don't care.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:36.583
There it is, people.

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:40.371
I like your customer service.
You got to write the rules.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.133
Oh, this is cool. Look at this.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.970
-So…
-What a place.

00:14:45.971 --> 00:14:49.171
All the history and look,
you have all the different fresh water.

00:14:49.455 --> 00:14:50.520
There we go.

00:14:51.078 --> 00:14:53.057
-Lisa, you want to show him
how you do a backflip?

00:14:53.057 --> 00:14:54.411
I'mma tell you what, I can do it.

00:14:54.411 --> 00:14:55.817
-No, seriously.
-Seriously?

00:14:55.817 --> 00:14:58.131
I don't want to get in trouble, though.

00:14:58.131 --> 00:15:00.540
-Hester made a complaint.
-You did it on Saturday.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:01.540
[scoffs]

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:03.350
[Peter] You did the backflip?

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:07.925
The biker that asked me to do it,
he brings groups of people.

00:15:08.477 --> 00:15:10.318
So, one day,
they're all sitting out on the patio.

00:15:10.318 --> 00:15:11.990
Must be 25, 30 of them.

00:15:12.572 --> 00:15:14.372
And I did it, and they all loved it.

00:15:14.852 --> 00:15:16.802
So, now, every time he comes in here…

00:15:19.225 --> 00:15:21.557
I try to sneak one in
every once in a while.

00:15:22.396 --> 00:15:23.856
I have a little over a month.

00:15:23.856 --> 00:15:27.140
Somewhere around March 10th,
I'm going to be a great-grandmother.

00:15:27.439 --> 00:15:31.126
-Wow.
-I turned 60 on Veterans Day, 11-11.

00:15:31.765 --> 00:15:35.870
And I wanted to be a great-grandmother
by the time I was 60 and I got my wish.

00:15:35.871 --> 00:15:38.210
-I'll be a great-grandmother…
-Congrats.

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:39.975
Somewhere around March 10th.

00:15:39.975 --> 00:15:41.175
What's the math on that?

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:43.378
-What do you mean on what?
-[Peter laughs]

00:15:43.378 --> 00:15:44.884
When did you have your first kid?

00:15:44.884 --> 00:15:47.744
I had my first child
the day I turned 15 and a half.

00:15:48.037 --> 00:15:49.037
Wow.

00:15:49.363 --> 00:15:50.530
I have two kids.

00:15:50.531 --> 00:15:52.170
I had the second one when I was 31.

00:15:52.171 --> 00:15:54.171
We're just rambling now waiting for you.

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.230
-My hands are freezing.
-It's great to see you.

00:15:57.230 --> 00:15:59.349
-Terry. Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

00:15:59.576 --> 00:16:01.026
-Thank you, Lisa.
-Absolutely.

00:16:01.322 --> 00:16:02.530
This is your restaurant?

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:04.010
Yes, it was my mom's.

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:06.010
-It was your mom's?
-Yes, she started it.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.300
-Oh, very cool.
-I can't take that credit.

00:16:08.668 --> 00:16:12.137
This is Gator Joe. He passed away,
but he used to sit out on the porch

00:16:12.137 --> 00:16:14.103
and tell stories of the Everglades.

00:16:14.103 --> 00:16:16.036
And he would have his panther out there.

00:16:16.036 --> 00:16:19.036
And he'd jump right back in the canal
and fight an alligator.

00:16:19.519 --> 00:16:21.378
-Out of the back side of the restaurant?
-Yes.

00:16:21.378 --> 00:16:24.102
-Gator Joe.
-It didn't matter. Look at him right there.

00:16:24.102 --> 00:16:27.702
He's got a cigarette between his hands
and a stick and he's in the woods.

00:16:28.236 --> 00:16:32.082
Ochopee used to be the dream town to be

00:16:32.636 --> 00:16:33.636
back in the day.

00:16:33.915 --> 00:16:35.803
-It was going to be bigger than Naples.
-Really?

00:16:35.803 --> 00:16:38.866
There was a time out here
where there were more people living

00:16:39.473 --> 00:16:42.430
back in the early 1900s than Naples.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:44.556
-So Terry, from my understanding,

00:16:44.556 --> 00:16:47.106
you can't really develop
out in these parts too easily.

00:16:47.106 --> 00:16:48.770
So there are only a few places?

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.110
Only what's existing.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:53.133
-Only what's existing?
-You can't buy property and build anything.

00:16:53.133 --> 00:16:55.030
This is the Big Cyprus National Park.

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.770
Big Cyprus and then we have
Everglades National Park.

00:16:58.367 --> 00:16:59.860
-Nobody's building there.
-No.

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:02.280
-So you're like in this oasis.
-I am.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:04.524
Of nature in a crowded state.

00:17:04.524 --> 00:17:05.524
Absolutely.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:09.162
My great grandfather
and grandfather on my mother's side

00:17:09.162 --> 00:17:12.494
came out here and bought property
out in Everglades City.

00:17:13.016 --> 00:17:17.093
And we have been coming out
since we were babies.

00:17:17.377 --> 00:17:19.870
Eventually my mom said,
"Okay, this is enough.

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:21.243
I'm done with Miami.

00:17:21.243 --> 00:17:22.843
Carl, we're going to the Glades."

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:25.723
And they bought this in the early '70s.

00:17:25.723 --> 00:17:28.710
My dad was a mechanic,
so he had a gas station

00:17:28.718 --> 00:17:30.368
and he was the mechanic out here.

00:17:30.917 --> 00:17:34.020
-So other than
your business there, Joanie's ,

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:38.537
things are sort of going downhill
as far as businesses, that sort of thing?

00:17:38.537 --> 00:17:41.870
-Or what's the story out here?
-There are no businesses really out here.

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:42.977
Yeah, okay.

00:17:42.977 --> 00:17:46.391
This closed up many years ago
when my mom had to pull up all the tanks.

00:17:46.391 --> 00:17:49.991
She couldn't afford it. She had to
get a loan to pull out six huge tanks.

00:17:50.706 --> 00:17:54.032
You know, when they were doing that
all back about 30 years ago.

00:17:54.032 --> 00:17:56.510
-Oh, when they had to change the tanks.
-Yes, yes.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:57.852
So it never reopened.

00:17:58.309 --> 00:18:01.978
And we are now
in the process of trying to

00:18:02.578 --> 00:18:04.710
take care of it and restore it.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:05.710
Okay.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:09.697
So we don't lose it because if you lose
the building, you can't rebuild it.

00:18:09.697 --> 00:18:12.487
-Those are the rules out here?
-Yeah, you can't rebuild anything.

00:18:12.487 --> 00:18:14.339
This is original.

00:18:14.339 --> 00:18:16.321
We want to take care of it. Just like that.

00:18:16.321 --> 00:18:20.150
That's a, you know, work in progress.
Always, it's a hundred years old.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:22.034
-You got your own world out here.
-We do.

00:18:22.101 --> 00:18:25.459
-So when you think Miami, St. Peters…
-You can keep Miami.

00:18:25.459 --> 00:18:26.459
Okay.

00:18:26.958 --> 00:18:29.472
-[Harrison] Where were you born?
-I was born in Hialeah.

00:18:29.472 --> 00:18:31.730
-Okay.
-But Hialeah had cows then.

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:32.771
Okay. Right, right.

00:18:32.771 --> 00:18:34.417
I mean, it's not like that now.

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:37.490
[Spanish]

00:18:40.397 --> 00:18:42.649
-Okay, you're Cuban descent?
-Si.

00:18:43.577 --> 00:18:45.630
-I am good old American Gringo.
-Gringo.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:46.880
Yeah, but you heard that.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:49.049
Your accent sounds good.

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.250
But I moved to Europe
when I was a young girl

00:18:52.001 --> 00:18:53.750
in Spain, in the Basque country.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:55.830
-Oh, okay.
-And I learned Spanish there.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:58.590
And my Spanish is… I don't know.

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.745
-You have a lisp?
-A little more eloquent.

00:19:00.745 --> 00:19:02.402
-You have a little lisp.
-It's Castillian.

00:19:02.402 --> 00:19:03.737
-Yeah, sure. Okay.

00:19:03.737 --> 00:19:06.181
-So you've seen a lot of the world.
-Yes. Well…

00:19:06.181 --> 00:19:08.515
How is… Why come back to this?

00:19:09.115 --> 00:19:11.463
People would think,
why live in the middle of a hot swamp?

00:19:11.463 --> 00:19:13.763
-[Harrison] That's the question. 
-Oh.

00:19:13.763 --> 00:19:15.714
When you figure that one out… [laughs]

00:19:16.075 --> 00:19:18.275
Because there is
no place like this on Earth.

00:19:19.071 --> 00:19:20.521
-There really is not.
-How so?

00:19:20.785 --> 00:19:22.830
It's just quaint. It's beautiful.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:24.298
It's rough. It's raw.

00:19:24.764 --> 00:19:28.651
A lot of people,
including myself until recently,

00:19:29.171 --> 00:19:31.150
didn't know that Florida
was such a frontier.

00:19:31.151 --> 00:19:32.450
Everyone thinks West.

00:19:32.451 --> 00:19:34.674
-I know.
-Everyone thinks Western migration, right?

00:19:34.674 --> 00:19:36.270
But Florida was actually the last.

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:37.686
It was after California.

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:39.210
Let me tell you something.

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:42.600
When I was reading A Land Remembered,
I thought… cowboys?

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:44.479
Tough out West?

00:19:44.479 --> 00:19:46.405
No, you are not tough until you have

00:19:48.005 --> 00:19:49.229
roughed the swamps

00:19:50.329 --> 00:19:52.840
in a hurricane, tying yourself to a tree.

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:54.580
'Cause you don't know a hurricane's coming.

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:56.591
They didn't know when
the bad weather was coming.

00:19:56.591 --> 00:19:59.641
They'd be moving cattle
through the swamps with mosquitoes and

00:20:00.289 --> 00:20:01.289
all that…

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:03.351
All those clothes. Women included, eh?

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:07.208
[Peter] What do we got here,
Harrison?

00:20:07.208 --> 00:20:08.890
So, this is a whole soft shell crab.

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:11.510
The whole with shell on,
fried it in the sandwich.

00:20:11.926 --> 00:20:13.732
-Super fresh.
-[Lisa] What do you think, guys?

00:20:13.732 --> 00:20:16.570
-I'm starting right now, Lisa..
-You're gonna love it.

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:18.063
Tell them about that soft shell crab.

00:20:18.063 --> 00:20:21.304
I'm going to take something that ain't
nothing but good. Nice and crunchy.

00:20:21.304 --> 00:20:24.330
Good. We batter it real good
but the batter's not thick.

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:26.160
-Okay.
-You can't pull it off.

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.087
But you talk about good.

00:20:28.649 --> 00:20:29.649
Mm-mm.

00:20:30.648 --> 00:20:34.245
I always suggest to people
that you get one soft shell sandwich

00:20:34.245 --> 00:20:35.810
and one crab cake sandwich.

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:37.710
Then you cut them in half
and holler, "Switch."

00:20:38.534 --> 00:20:39.934
-Switcharoo.
-Next time.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:43.710
-All right, Harrison.
-What do you think, first bite analysis?

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:48.010
-Mmm.
-Decent.

00:20:49.348 --> 00:20:50.490
This is fantastic.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:52.011
This is really good.

00:20:52.953 --> 00:20:56.166
Excellent meat. The bun is good. Very soft.

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:58.450
Yeah. No. It's the real deal.

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.550
Yeah, look at that.

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:01.990
-Grouper, right?
-Grouper, yeah.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.090
-Blackened.
-Blackened right out of the Gulf.

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:07.400
Delicious. About as good and tasty
as a fish as you can get.

00:21:08.225 --> 00:21:09.225
[country music]

00:21:21.619 --> 00:21:22.982
[Peter] What are we coming up on?

00:21:22.982 --> 00:21:24.489
[Harrison] Clyde Butcher's gallery.

00:21:24.489 --> 00:21:27.031
Legendary photographer
known all over the world.

00:21:27.478 --> 00:21:30.276
So gallery out in the middle
of the Everglades?

00:21:30.276 --> 00:21:31.891
-Yes. A photo gallery.

00:21:31.891 --> 00:21:35.691
And not just some photos, but the best…
Hey look, there's Clyde as a mailbox.

00:21:36.926 --> 00:21:40.392
The best kind.
So we'll get in there now and we'll delve.

00:21:40.933 --> 00:21:41.933
[music continues]

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:51.477
-John, you work here at the gallery?
-That is correct.

00:21:51.477 --> 00:21:53.360
-Okay.
-I'm an environmentalist.

00:21:53.361 --> 00:21:57.784
I've been doing a lot of backpack
and hiking here for 35 years.

00:21:57.784 --> 00:22:00.200
Out here, it's a deciduous coniferous.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:02.660
So the trees are not dead.
They've lost their needles.

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:04.323
-We're going to get out there too, right?

00:22:04.323 --> 00:22:06.997
You are going to go out there
and I'm going to show you a vision.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:11.035
If you had a time machine
and brought it back 12 to 13,000 years ago,

00:22:11.035 --> 00:22:12.369
the ocean would be over here.

00:22:12.369 --> 00:22:13.875
We'd be a hundred miles wider.

00:22:13.875 --> 00:22:15.000
-Okay?
-Okay.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:17.402
So we have been like this
for about 8,000 years

00:22:17.402 --> 00:22:19.486
with the vegetation types that we have.

00:22:19.486 --> 00:22:21.636
-South Florida, Miami's over here.
-Correct.

00:22:21.976 --> 00:22:26.203
And this is what we looked like before
people as far as Caucasians got involved.

00:22:26.218 --> 00:22:27.424
Up here is Orlando.

00:22:27.713 --> 00:22:29.580
And this is Kissimmee River

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:31.810
filling the lake from here to here
is a hundred miles.

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:34.982
The water was so slow
from 14 feet of sea level to nothing,

00:22:34.982 --> 00:22:36.732
it took a year to make the journey.

00:22:37.385 --> 00:22:39.984
And these islands right here
are mountains,

00:22:39.984 --> 00:22:42.565
which are called hammocks,
about a meter tall.

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:45.221
And then you've got bay heads
with their kinds of plants

00:22:45.221 --> 00:22:46.321
and then the prairies.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:49.560
And then what you have
are pinelands where Miama is now.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:51.473
-And you've got cypress tree.
-Miama?

00:22:51.473 --> 00:22:54.977
Miama is what the old timers…
If you were to read Land Remembered

00:22:55.413 --> 00:22:58.939
and you talked to an old timer
and you said Miami, they'd be pissed off.

00:22:58.939 --> 00:22:59.820
But if you said…

00:23:00.307 --> 00:23:01.334
Edit that.

00:23:01.334 --> 00:23:04.047
But if you presented
this to somebody and you said Miama,

00:23:04.054 --> 00:23:05.525
they'll smile and be happy with you.

00:23:05.525 --> 00:23:06.875
Isn't it the Miami Indians?

00:23:07.255 --> 00:23:08.255
No. Well,

00:23:08.907 --> 00:23:10.115
Miami's in Ohio.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.583
Miama is what
the old timers called it here.

00:23:12.583 --> 00:23:13.640
Okay, gotcha.

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:15.040
-Okay, so this is the old version.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:16.127
Correct. This is current.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:18.920
-Okay, here we go.
-And they couldn't grow any crops.

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:19.970
This is all cropland.

00:23:20.566 --> 00:23:23.975
1926, a hurricane came up here,
blue water killed 300.

00:23:25.186 --> 00:23:27.952
1928, another unknown hurricane.

00:23:27.952 --> 00:23:31.857
It came up here, Cat 5,
and it killed about 2,500 people.

00:23:32.267 --> 00:23:35.567
So they built a dyke around this,
and instead of the water coming here,

00:23:36.021 --> 00:23:37.103
it comes through here.

00:23:37.103 --> 00:23:39.173
-And the St. Lucie Canal.
-Oh, I gotcha.

00:23:39.174 --> 00:23:40.174
Okay.

00:23:40.353 --> 00:23:44.800
So, this area here
was proposed as a national park

00:23:45.213 --> 00:23:47.363
by Ernest Coe,
the father of the Everglades.

00:23:47.364 --> 00:23:50.065
And when he proposed it,
they thought he was insane.

00:23:50.065 --> 00:23:52.185
So he brought 'em down,
put him in the Goodyear blimp,

00:23:52.185 --> 00:23:53.873
flew them over the proposed area,

00:23:53.873 --> 00:23:56.150
and they were so enamored
by it in the '20s.

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:58.000
But there was no money in the 1930s.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.171
We had the Depression.

00:23:59.171 --> 00:24:02.529
Congress okayed it in '34,
but it wasn't until 1947

00:24:02.529 --> 00:24:04.729
became a park
because of plants and animals.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:06.930
And no one had ever done that before.

00:24:07.262 --> 00:24:08.262
Brand new.

00:24:08.262 --> 00:24:09.968
This out here are mines.

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:12.060
They couldn't grow crops there.

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:13.200
The crops would not grow.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:14.440
They were absent nutrients.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.040
And this is one of the hints.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:17.540
And those are phosphate mines.

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:20.867
And the other problem is
we were shy on nitrogen.

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:23.075
So when they added
phosphate and nitrogen,

00:24:23.075 --> 00:24:26.075
they could grow their plants,
but it impacted the rest of us.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.239
The rain system that used to be
is now different.

00:24:29.239 --> 00:24:30.838
We're canal driven primarily.

00:24:31.127 --> 00:24:35.127
And Highway 41 and I-75 are dams.

00:24:35.127 --> 00:24:37.420
-And we're right around here?
-We are here, correct.

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:38.176
Okay.

00:24:38.176 --> 00:24:39.426
And this is the jet port.

00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:43.638
Now when Ernest Coe didn't want to come
to Everglades City

00:24:43.638 --> 00:24:45.975
for the dedication in 1947 in December,

00:24:46.449 --> 00:24:49.610
because they had cut chunks
of what the proposed park was.

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:50.610
This is just a mere…

00:24:51.329 --> 00:24:54.179
This is what… We get the leftovers
of whatever's above us.

00:24:54.414 --> 00:24:56.762
But if we got the water here
that's been enriched

00:24:56.762 --> 00:24:59.400
because of phosphate and nitrate,
it would kill us.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.246
So there are areas out here
that they've been trying to

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.282
have settling dams, pretty much.

00:25:05.282 --> 00:25:08.956
Areas, and you can have plants
that can extract the phosphate and nitrate.

00:25:08.956 --> 00:25:11.808
What I want to show you here
is the reason this happened

00:25:12.448 --> 00:25:14.274
got cut back is because of greed.

00:25:14.706 --> 00:25:17.660
And then Coe wasn't gonna come
to this dedication.

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:18.660
But later on,

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:20.970
we did this.

00:25:21.187 --> 00:25:22.230
This is what happened.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:25.490
This is why we have
the Big Cypress Preserve is this.

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:27.804
This area here is the jet port.

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:30.649
It was a training jet port between
Miami-Dade

00:25:30.955 --> 00:25:33.569
or Miama-Dade and Collier County.

00:25:33.975 --> 00:25:38.125
And this is going to have a city of 10 to
100,000 people and that's why Leon's here.

00:25:38.376 --> 00:25:40.572
Because he was buying land in swampland.

00:25:40.572 --> 00:25:43.118
-Who's Leon?
-Leon that originally owned this.

00:25:43.439 --> 00:25:45.360
-Okay, this property here.
-Yeah.

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:48.393
The buildings are all on land
that he had a footprint on.

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:50.660
[Peter] Okay.

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:52.352
-So Clyde Butcher…
-Clyde Butcher.

00:25:52.352 --> 00:25:53.452
Who came out here when?

00:25:53.452 --> 00:25:55.125
Just before Hurricane Andrew.

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:57.202
-When was that?
-'92.

00:25:57.202 --> 00:25:58.520
-'92, correct.
-Okay.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.814
They bought it from Leon
who had bought land

00:26:00.814 --> 00:26:03.523
because he was speculating
that he would make a mint.

00:26:03.523 --> 00:26:06.851
with all the people that were going to live
in this area because of the jet port.

00:26:06.858 --> 00:26:09.412
When Clyde and Nicky came out here,
they said, man, you're nuts.

00:26:09.412 --> 00:26:11.590
You think you can build a gallery out here?

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:14.333
They said, you must have
a loose screw somewhere.

00:26:15.093 --> 00:26:16.900
And there's loose screw sanctuary.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:20.640
So he was being investigated because
a lot of drugs ran through the area.

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:23.000
And they assumed
he was doing something illegal.

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:23.770
-Oh.

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:25.742
And they found out over the years that,

00:26:25.742 --> 00:26:28.280
no, he was perfectly legal
and he was perfectly sane.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:31.998
And he's won many awards
from Ansel Adams to national recognition

00:26:31.998 --> 00:26:36.122
for the work he has done in showing
this environment that is so foreign

00:26:36.122 --> 00:26:38.772
that most people
will stand on the outside looking in.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:42.373
But I have the privilege of taking people
into it to show them what it offers.

00:26:42.373 --> 00:26:42.926
-Okay.

00:26:42.926 --> 00:26:44.243
And what it offers is quiet

00:26:44.963 --> 00:26:48.427
where you can just take a breath
and listen to nothing.

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:50.668
You put away all your cell phones.

00:26:51.055 --> 00:26:54.736
But with me and others,
Scott and Connie and Trish, we do walks.

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:57.070
You have to live in the moment.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:59.555
Each step determines
whether you're successful or not.

00:26:59.556 --> 00:27:02.656
And some of the people I've worked with
are very good at tactile walking.

00:27:02.656 --> 00:27:03.706
They can feel things.

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:05.280
Others, not so much.

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:07.264
If you get distracted,
you end up falling in

00:27:07.264 --> 00:27:09.130
and I grade you by your splash.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:11.120
If you make a big splash,
you get less points.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:12.264
I want a clean entry.

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:15.360
We have alligators here that tolerate us.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.780
They've known us for years and we don't
harass them and we respect them.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:20.937
So when we get by them, we speak quietly.

00:27:21.300 --> 00:27:22.078
-Okay.

00:27:22.078 --> 00:27:23.728
Mama's got baby gators over here.

00:27:24.126 --> 00:27:26.440
And everything eats baby alligators.

00:27:26.714 --> 00:27:29.375
And if the baby alligators are in trouble,
they call mom.

00:27:29.375 --> 00:27:30.863
[imitates gator grunting sound]

00:27:30.863 --> 00:27:32.929
And she'll come and she'll protect them.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:35.318
Usually male alligators eat them,

00:27:35.318 --> 00:27:37.619
but she has a relationship
with the male alligator here

00:27:37.619 --> 00:27:42.065
where she actually would guide him away
from the babies over the years.

00:27:42.065 --> 00:27:45.680
And then finally last year
she was letting them lay on his back.

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:46.722
Okay.

00:27:46.722 --> 00:27:48.978
So alligators have different personalities.

00:27:49.829 --> 00:27:50.881
Just to let you know.

00:27:50.881 --> 00:27:52.742
-So this one…
-We're going to go see this one.

00:27:52.743 --> 00:27:55.330
I'm going to respect her.
If I see her, I'll show you.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:56.330
I'll talk quietly.

00:27:56.685 --> 00:27:58.920
And if we see the babies, it'll be special.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.309
Lot of times,
this is where it's rented up here.

00:28:01.805 --> 00:28:04.030
This is where Clyde and Niki used to live.

00:28:04.030 --> 00:28:05.280
And now they rent it out.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:08.305
And when you do,
you get a visual of a morning.

00:28:08.725 --> 00:28:10.240
I call this mental floss.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:11.540
You get your brain cleared

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:14.250
because of all the garbage
you put up with two-legged animals.

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:16.000
The snakes and the gators are cool.

00:28:16.001 --> 00:28:20.610
As long as you're away from the ones
that have been fed by people by the road.

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:21.610
They're dangerous.

00:28:22.235 --> 00:28:24.476
And you can't move…
A fed gator is a dead gator.

00:28:24.476 --> 00:28:27.070
So you never feed them
because it becomes a problem.

00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:29.645
So what's going to happen?
I'm going to go over here quietly

00:28:29.645 --> 00:28:32.295
and we're going to see
if Mama's there and her babies.

00:28:36.248 --> 00:28:37.790
[whispers] There's Mama.

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:39.880
She's pointed this direction in the water.

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:40.953
[Peter whispers] Oh yeah.

00:28:40.953 --> 00:28:43.489
Her babies may be by.
I'm not going to get in her space.

00:28:43.489 --> 00:28:46.583
But that's where she is
and this is where she had her nest.

00:28:46.583 --> 00:28:48.801
Last year she had a nest up in trees,

00:28:49.253 --> 00:28:50.653
but it got raided by a bear.

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:53.390
This is where her nest is this year.

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:58.300
And this is an alligator egg.

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:02.087
And I'd never approached this if she was
nearby here. That's an alligator egg.

00:29:02.087 --> 00:29:04.810
And when the eggs are laid,
the sex hasn't been determined.

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:08.190
What determines the sex of the alligator
is the temperature of the nest.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:10.673
It's the opposite
of what happens with turtles.

00:29:10.673 --> 00:29:13.623
So sea turtles are now being hatched
that are mostly female.

00:29:14.025 --> 00:29:17.040
Which the question comes in 30, 40 years,
it's not going to be

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.240
are there going to be
males to fertilize them?

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:20.840
So you'd assume that's this way.

00:29:20.846 --> 00:29:22.827
But in this reptile, for alligators,

00:29:22.828 --> 00:29:24.886
the warm eggs become males, not females.

00:29:25.631 --> 00:29:27.873
What about this water?
You said it's actually very clean.

00:29:27.873 --> 00:29:29.220
We're in the dry season.

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:32.263
It is brown because of tannic acid
when the leaves decay.

00:29:32.263 --> 00:29:33.263
But it's very clean?

00:29:33.701 --> 00:29:35.883
Incredibly clean. We're gonna have
the different senses

00:29:35.883 --> 00:29:37.709
and you're going to find out
how clean it is.

00:29:37.776 --> 00:29:38.776
[splashing]

00:29:40.247 --> 00:29:42.175
This has been trod upon
to the point that it's…

00:29:42.176 --> 00:29:44.320
-Oh, it's cold.
-Oh, well, it's brisk.

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:44.986
Yeah.

00:29:44.992 --> 00:29:47.179
If you feel warm,
it's either you're hypothermic

00:29:47.179 --> 00:29:49.213
or somebody urinated by you.

00:29:49.213 --> 00:29:50.213
[laughter]

00:29:52.599 --> 00:29:54.119
[Harrison] I think the water is nice.

00:29:54.119 --> 00:29:55.819
-It's incredible.
-Yeah.

00:29:55.819 --> 00:29:57.285
I didn't think it would be so clean.

00:29:57.285 --> 00:29:59.270
Of course not.
See, because you came with baggage.

00:29:59.270 --> 00:30:01.870
You came with the ideas
of what this would represent.

00:30:02.856 --> 00:30:04.000
[Peter] Swamp baggage?

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.440
This actually turns out,
it is called Ladies of the Night.

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:08.943
It's a Leon Orchid.

00:30:08.943 --> 00:30:10.834
It has smell only at night.

00:30:11.476 --> 00:30:14.280
And that's to attract the pollinators,
which are moths.

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:15.908
This is another orchid right here.

00:30:15.908 --> 00:30:18.840
And if you touch it,
it's called Epidendrum Rigidum.

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:20.004
It's a rigid orchid.

00:30:20.004 --> 00:30:21.004
-Oh, yeah.

00:30:21.004 --> 00:30:22.950
-Very tactile, right?
-Yeah.

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:24.850
All this is food that's on the orchid.

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:26.900
This is a pond apple tree.

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:30.600
Around Lake Okeechobee, the pond
apples were seven, eight feet diameter.

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:31.763
They were huge.

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:34.153
-So Clyde and Nicky bought the land.
-Mm-hmm.

00:30:34.153 --> 00:30:36.521
-But they'd been all over the world, right?
-Correct.

00:30:36.521 --> 00:30:38.489
Lived many places around the US.

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:40.480
And why'd they decide on here?

00:30:40.481 --> 00:30:42.410
There are different ways of healing.

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:45.080
They had lost their son to a drunk driver.

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:46.075
Okay.

00:30:46.075 --> 00:30:48.884
And when they were searching
to how to sort their life out,

00:30:48.884 --> 00:30:52.488
they came here and they
found a place that was quiet

00:30:52.488 --> 00:30:55.395
and away from the populace.

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:59.740
And they felt the calmness and the beauty
of what they were experiencing.

00:31:00.020 --> 00:31:03.781
So when they came in here and opened up
their photography opportunity

00:31:04.194 --> 00:31:05.887
and it started getting popular,

00:31:05.887 --> 00:31:08.587
then they realized
that this is something to be shared.

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:11.573
And we have a sixth grade program
that we do.

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:13.400
And I help with the fundraiser

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:16.515
and the people that come here
pay money to go on swamp walks

00:31:16.516 --> 00:31:18.842
on any particular day when Clyde is here.

00:31:18.842 --> 00:31:21.582
And then the money this year,
they made $40,000

00:31:21.582 --> 00:31:23.298
from the environmental
education program.

00:31:23.298 --> 00:31:25.612
So any sixth grader
in Collier County

00:31:25.612 --> 00:31:28.462
can go on a swamp walk
because of the monies that are made.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:30.720
You are seeing this at a point in time.

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:33.233
You know a picture captures the moment

00:31:34.026 --> 00:31:35.576
and it may never be seen again.

00:31:35.838 --> 00:31:37.838
But what we're gonna have a problem with

00:31:37.838 --> 00:31:41.223
is within 20 years
it is going to look a lot different.

00:31:42.157 --> 00:31:44.674
This particular tree will be gone,
this species.

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:46.403
-How do you know?

00:31:46.403 --> 00:31:48.437
Because there is something
called the ash borer

00:31:48.437 --> 00:31:52.180
that is eating the ash trees
in the Midwest.

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:55.530
And as it moves down here,
it is going to consume this.

00:31:55.862 --> 00:32:00.403
This is what we find the predominance
of our orchid species on, on ash trees.

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:04.144
So you are seeing this now as it is,
it is going to be different.

00:32:06.856 --> 00:32:09.025
It is spongier because
it hasn't been destroyed

00:32:09.025 --> 00:32:10.580
by thousands of feet.

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:11.580
-Yeah, right?

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:13.740
Now we're coming up to the hat rack turn.

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:16.500
[Peter] Much different zone out here.

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:18.230
What do you feel when you came here?

00:32:18.230 --> 00:32:19.230
You felt the sponge?

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:21.700
Number two, you felt the heat?

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:22.700
-Mm-hmm.

00:32:22.700 --> 00:32:24.820
And what you also see is
the trees are smaller.

00:32:24.820 --> 00:32:27.220
Then I ask people why are
these smaller trees out here?

00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:29.760
These, many of these are just as old
as the big ones we saw.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:31.465
-What makes this different?
-Less moisture?

00:32:31.465 --> 00:32:32.965
Less moisture, less nutrients.

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:35.954
So this is gonna be completely crunchy dry.

00:32:35.954 --> 00:32:38.908
And this material here that looks lumpy
and uninteresting

00:32:38.908 --> 00:32:42.505
is the most important aspect to the health
of the Everglades right here.

00:32:42.505 --> 00:32:44.744
If you smell it, it smells so clean,

00:32:45.636 --> 00:32:46.800
it's going to blow your mind.

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:48.450
-Oh, yeah.
-It's not stinky swamp.

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:51.806
And that periphyton has over 300 kinds
of algae in it

00:32:52.421 --> 00:32:53.471
that are green algae.

00:32:53.792 --> 00:32:57.614
They produce oxygen and they are food
for a major animal out here.

00:32:58.176 --> 00:32:59.439
[Peter] Crush it and smell it?

00:32:59.439 --> 00:33:00.339
[John] Smell that.

00:33:01.886 --> 00:33:03.032
Oh, yeah.

00:33:03.032 --> 00:33:05.060
You know what?
It's called lemon bacopa.

00:33:05.060 --> 00:33:07.198
Why is it important?
You smear it on yourself.

00:33:07.198 --> 00:33:08.848
No, not this one. Smell that one.

00:33:09.173 --> 00:33:11.312
I talk to the plants that I don't want to,

00:33:11.312 --> 00:33:13.310
you know, I just
use permission from them.

00:33:13.310 --> 00:33:15.308
This is something you also be…

00:33:15.309 --> 00:33:16.896
That is a mosquito repellent.

00:33:16.896 --> 00:33:17.896
-Oh, cool.
-Okay.

00:33:17.896 --> 00:33:19.900
This one also is a mosquito eater.

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:21.110
It's called bladderwort.

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:23.875
And the little bladders that are in here
will suck in insects

00:33:23.875 --> 00:33:25.137
and use it as nitrogen.

00:33:26.532 --> 00:33:28.400
[John] I want you to see
a big cypress tree.

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:30.500
-You want to see a big cypress tree?
-Yeah.

00:33:31.374 --> 00:33:33.447
I'll show you one of my beauties.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:36.415
Now, tactile walking,
you're taking your time.

00:33:36.415 --> 00:33:38.316
There are things under the water
you can't see.

00:33:38.316 --> 00:33:40.325
Things under the water you can't see.

00:33:40.658 --> 00:33:43.338
You can feel it.
Trust your gut is telling you.

00:33:46.484 --> 00:33:48.620
I don't know if the camera
is catching this.

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:50.758
It's not fully, obviously, but,

00:33:51.785 --> 00:33:55.112
it is a totally different universe in here.

00:33:55.780 --> 00:33:57.433
-You know what, John?
-Sure.

00:33:57.433 --> 00:33:59.624
You said you lived in the desert
for a little bit.

00:33:59.624 --> 00:34:01.524
-I did.
-First impressions, the desert…

00:34:02.007 --> 00:34:04.380
a lot of people think, yeah,
there's not much here, right?

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:05.534
It's the desert.

00:34:05.534 --> 00:34:07.975
You give it time. You feel its rhythms.

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:11.588
You see the trickles of water
between the…

00:34:12.925 --> 00:34:14.132
The ravines.

00:34:14.538 --> 00:34:15.560
It opens up.

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:17.045
And I feel like the swamp

00:34:17.808 --> 00:34:18.808
is the same.

00:34:18.808 --> 00:34:22.247
You might drive through and say,
"Hey, there's not much here."

00:34:23.181 --> 00:34:24.862
And then once you go granular,

00:34:25.502 --> 00:34:27.337
it starts getting very interesting.

00:34:27.337 --> 00:34:28.337
-Yeah.

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:32.607
This tree represents wisdom.

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:37.420
It has stood the test of time for hundreds,
maybe a thousand… or hundreds of years.

00:34:37.723 --> 00:34:38.723
Look at this.

00:34:39.410 --> 00:34:42.855
This just screams how beautiful this is.

00:34:44.382 --> 00:34:45.973
Again, the water level would be here,

00:34:46.546 --> 00:34:49.260
but this cypress has experienced a lot.

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:51.931
And it's in the middle…
So a dome is very stable.

00:34:51.931 --> 00:34:54.916
So when winds blow over here,
they hold each other together.

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:57.340
This is one of my trees.

00:34:57.340 --> 00:34:59.946
Others have their trees
they find particularly attractive.

00:35:00.388 --> 00:35:03.040
But I just like the size
and I'd like you guys to stand next to it.

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:06.020
And give me a cell and I'll take a picture
or whatever you got.

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:08.209
I'll take a picture of
both you guys standing there.

00:35:08.209 --> 00:35:09.840
Peter, you get the energy first.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:10.840
-Pretending like you…

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:13.291
-How long does he have to
stand next to it to get the energy?

00:35:13.291 --> 00:35:16.046
No, you know what?
The Miccosukees and Seminoles,

00:35:16.046 --> 00:35:18.571
they actually form bands
around these trees.

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:20.620
And because of the energy they have.

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:22.020
Go.

00:35:22.087 --> 00:35:24.680
You are communing with
a tree of knowledge.

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:29.170
You can think of it as an ent if you want,
from Lord of the Rings.

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:33.044
Just the point that these things
are incredible creatures.

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.500
And when they get cut,
they don't last anymore.

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:37.712
-You and I talked about redwoods.
-Yeah.

00:35:37.712 --> 00:35:41.465
The redwoods were preserved by the
Redwoods League, which were evil people.

00:35:41.465 --> 00:35:43.080
They were cutting redwoods. Why?

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:45.906
They wouldn't save them
because they were using them for pipes

00:35:45.906 --> 00:35:49.010
for waste water and that's before
they had metal pipes.

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:51.333
And also Transcontinental Railroad.

00:35:51.333 --> 00:35:52.580
-Oh, you got it.

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:55.880
A lot of California was decimated
because they were mining,

00:35:55.880 --> 00:36:00.223
especially Nevada
for things like silver and gold.

00:36:00.790 --> 00:36:03.060
Wow, look at the light.
I just love how the light plays.

00:36:03.061 --> 00:36:06.111
-I know. That's the thing.
See, that's what I want you to see.

00:36:10.473 --> 00:36:11.466
That's nice.

00:36:11.466 --> 00:36:14.339
You are doing what I like to do
with people. I bring them out here

00:36:14.339 --> 00:36:16.710
and they close their eyes
and it's magic.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:17.934
You just hear your breath.

00:36:17.934 --> 00:36:19.784
You breathe through your nose slowly.

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:22.650
And we have the absence of the traffic.

00:36:22.946 --> 00:36:24.210
It's primeval.

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:25.490
It's a fantasy land.

00:36:25.491 --> 00:36:26.750
It's a connection.

00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:30.175
So when people go to Disney to see
the different hippopotamuses

00:36:30.175 --> 00:36:32.957
coming out of the river tour
and all that, I take them to reality.

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:36.220
To try to explain it is very difficult.

00:36:36.585 --> 00:36:38.619
The way to understand this
is to experience it.

00:36:38.619 --> 00:36:39.607
-Yeah.

00:36:39.607 --> 00:36:41.704
And all of the stereotypes
that you brought with you

00:36:41.704 --> 00:36:44.142
about the stinking swamp, the quicksand,

00:36:44.142 --> 00:36:45.759
there's no quicksand out here,

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:48.440
about the attacking alligators
which we've seen.

00:36:48.789 --> 00:36:50.302
All right. All the things.

00:36:50.302 --> 00:36:53.230
Back in the '40s, it was the Indians
that would take you out.

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:54.550
Not as a date.

00:36:54.550 --> 00:36:56.113
It was a final trip.

00:36:57.112 --> 00:37:00.776
So the things that we bring baggage-wise,
I like to remove that baggage

00:37:00.776 --> 00:37:03.358
and show you what
the true connections are out here.

00:37:03.358 --> 00:37:04.358
-I appreciate it.

00:37:04.358 --> 00:37:05.908
And you can be part of that by,

00:37:06.513 --> 00:37:08.635
like we just did,
and listen to nothing.

00:37:09.315 --> 00:37:11.730
-Which is the best.
-There's everything in the nothingness.

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:12.730
Correct.

00:37:13.797 --> 00:37:14.797
[silence]

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:26.105
[Peter] All right.
So here's the legend, Clyde Butcher.

00:37:26.562 --> 00:37:28.750
And you said this photography is amazing.

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:30.214
Yeah. You have to see it for yourself.

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:33.150
-Hey, guys.
-Hey, how you doing?

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:34.690
Good. How was the walk?

00:37:34.690 --> 00:37:36.154
-So cool.
-Spectacular.

00:37:36.154 --> 00:37:39.834
Clyde has been called the Ansel Adams
of the Everglades by some.

00:37:40.296 --> 00:37:43.351
He's traditional large-format
black-and-white photography.

00:37:43.669 --> 00:37:46.397
Film, prints,
made in the darkroom, handmade.

00:37:46.397 --> 00:37:47.550
Each handmade.

00:37:48.299 --> 00:37:49.749
[Peter] Oh, that's beautiful.

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:52.690
Wow.

00:37:52.690 --> 00:37:56.474
-So, you know, as a photographer too,
Clyde's just such a huge inspiration.

00:37:56.474 --> 00:37:59.683
-You're a photographer also?
-Yeah. I have work in here as well.

00:37:59.683 --> 00:38:02.483
-This is his wife's work?
-Yep. This is Niky Butcher, his wife.

00:38:02.483 --> 00:38:04.330
-And she does some color.
-Yep.

00:38:04.330 --> 00:38:07.370
So it's black-and-white photography,
and then she hand-colors it.

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:08.850
Oh, okay. I got you.

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:10.650
Yeah, it's got a surreal look to it.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:14.790
Very traditional Florida colors,
turquoise and pinks and yellows.

00:38:15.053 --> 00:38:17.261
-Okay, that's really interesting.

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:19.890
That's almost 3D. It's like coming to life.

00:38:20.283 --> 00:38:21.433
[Harrison] It's unique.

00:38:22.390 --> 00:38:23.890
-This is all your work, Andy?
-Yes.

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:25.050
-Nice.
-Wonderful.

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:26.343
Oh, that's a cool one.

00:38:26.343 --> 00:38:30.482
Yeah, that was in my head
for a long time, trying to…

00:38:30.731 --> 00:38:34.749
Had this thought of a mangrove
with a perfect reflection of a gator.

00:38:35.109 --> 00:38:37.309
-You're putting your time in
out there then.

00:38:37.765 --> 00:38:38.965
Getting shots like this.

00:38:39.711 --> 00:38:41.450
-That is so cool.
-Thank you.

00:38:41.450 --> 00:38:43.360
[Harrison] The roseate
with the babies too.

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:44.638
[Peter] I love this.

00:38:59.942 --> 00:39:01.808
[Peter] Now we're deep in
on the dirt roads.

00:39:01.808 --> 00:39:03.775
[Harrison] Deep in the Cypress.
Turner River Road.

00:39:03.842 --> 00:39:06.120
-But a few of these places, huh?

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:08.478
-Yes. And that is, again, the key…

00:39:08.478 --> 00:39:10.730
-"Bad dog. He thinks he's a lion."

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:12.277
I bet he does.

00:39:12.870 --> 00:39:15.385
That's the key difference
in the designation is that

00:39:15.385 --> 00:39:17.915
there's a lot of homestead camps out here
and hunting camps

00:39:17.915 --> 00:39:19.590
and people still living out here.

00:39:20.047 --> 00:39:22.789
That's a genuine Big Cypress swamp buggy.

00:39:23.286 --> 00:39:25.837
Unique vehicles made for this terrain.

00:39:25.837 --> 00:39:28.758
And if you see these old pictures
of them in the '20s and '30s,

00:39:28.758 --> 00:39:31.358
they'd have like Model A engines
and Model T engines.

00:39:31.955 --> 00:39:34.940
A lot of them have the tires
as tall as you and me, you know?

00:39:34.940 --> 00:39:37.202
-Oh, nice.
-Look at that thing. For the big Cypress.

00:39:37.202 --> 00:39:40.077
-Can we check it out?
-Yeah. Let's pull over and check it out.

00:39:45.756 --> 00:39:48.223
This is really interesting, guys.

00:39:48.917 --> 00:39:50.130
It's like Uganda.

00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:52.523
-Have you been to Uganda?
-I have.

00:39:52.590 --> 00:39:54.293
-Looks like this?
-Sort of.

00:39:54.870 --> 00:39:56.439
-Some areas.
-Yeah?

00:39:56.830 --> 00:39:57.830
Look at this thing.

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:00.630
-Do they have swamp buggies in Uganda?
-I don't think so.

00:40:01.255 --> 00:40:02.582
This thing is wild.

00:40:02.950 --> 00:40:05.890
Where the airboats can't go
or what's that all about?

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:10.040
Yeah, the water is deeper and more narrow
than the airboats can get through.

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:13.130
And then it also does a good job
of getting through the dryer patches

00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:14.503
that the airboats can't get to.

00:40:14.503 --> 00:40:15.503
-Oh, yeah.

00:40:15.503 --> 00:40:18.015
So it's like specific
for this environment, you know?

00:40:18.082 --> 00:40:19.082
[engine rattling]

00:40:24.622 --> 00:40:28.010
[Peter] We just saw this buggy
and we're like, it's so beautiful out here.

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:29.376
Someone's got to come back for it.

00:40:29.376 --> 00:40:30.343
It's pretty.

00:40:30.803 --> 00:40:31.803
-It is.

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:34.967
Where does one buy a buggy like this?

00:40:34.967 --> 00:40:36.250
-You don't. You build it.

00:40:36.250 --> 00:40:38.552
-You built these?
-Yeah. You build them.

00:40:38.552 --> 00:40:41.263
You found like an old truck chassis?

00:40:41.263 --> 00:40:42.863
A friend of mine built this one.

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:45.664
Hell, it's 35 years old.

00:40:46.375 --> 00:40:48.755
We've rolled it over. We got drunk.

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:53.820
There's been more deer hanging off of this
than most people seen in a lifetime.

00:40:54.352 --> 00:40:55.713
-Are you from out here?

00:40:55.713 --> 00:40:56.912
I'm from Chokoloskee.

00:40:57.387 --> 00:40:59.390
-Born and raised?
-Born and raised.

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:00.520
How's it been?

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:02.345
The earlier life was really better.

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:06.130
Things has changed so much
with the national parks and stuff.

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:09.508
We used to… fly free.

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:11.887
You know, we used to eat

00:41:13.293 --> 00:41:14.970
all the game we wanted.

00:41:15.528 --> 00:41:16.908
We killed deer.

00:41:17.620 --> 00:41:19.847
We killed, ate manatee.

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:21.310
We ate

00:41:21.900 --> 00:41:24.916
loggerhead turtles, green turtles.

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:28.244
We ate white ivis is what they call them.

00:41:28.244 --> 00:41:30.755
We call them Chokoloskee chickens, curlew.

00:41:30.755 --> 00:41:32.241
-We enjoyed ourselves.
-Oh, yeah.

00:41:32.241 --> 00:41:34.241
We didn't have no problems with the law.

00:41:34.757 --> 00:41:37.655
If anything, we'd kill a deer
and give them some. You know what I mean?

00:41:37.655 --> 00:41:38.855
They never mess with us.

00:41:39.450 --> 00:41:43.110
Okay. But I mean, it looks
pretty empty out here obviously.

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:45.630
-These parts, especially.
-I don't break the law no more.

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:47.450
-Yeah.
-I don't even drive fast.

00:41:47.450 --> 00:41:48.983
I don't… It ain't worth it.

00:41:49.629 --> 00:41:52.748
After getting in trouble
and doing the things I done,

00:41:52.749 --> 00:41:54.869
you know, I smuggled…

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:58.712
I was part of a family,
part of our family, and we smuggled

00:41:59.501 --> 00:42:01.263
millions of pounds of weed.

00:42:01.268 --> 00:42:02.268
I did 10 years.

00:42:02.268 --> 00:42:03.490
-No way. No way.

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:05.668
I did 10 years in federal prison.

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:08.834
Got my picture in Life Magazine.

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:12.257
You did 10 years in prison
for smuggling weed?

00:42:12.578 --> 00:42:13.578
10 years.

00:42:13.578 --> 00:42:15.944
So we heard that story from this morning.

00:42:16.285 --> 00:42:17.285
-In Copeland.

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:20.630
From Copeland they were talking about
you were running drugs.

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:22.750
Man, the money was just unbelievable.

00:42:22.750 --> 00:42:24.450
It was like something off of a TV.

00:42:25.276 --> 00:42:27.403
Like thousands and thousands a week.

00:42:27.403 --> 00:42:29.370
Hundreds of thousands of dollars.

00:42:29.370 --> 00:42:30.283
A month?

00:42:30.283 --> 00:42:31.550
Oh, a week.

00:42:31.550 --> 00:42:32.550
A day!

00:42:33.283 --> 00:42:34.343
-[laughter]
-C'mon!

00:42:34.343 --> 00:42:38.490
We had so much money, man,
it was giving it away.

00:42:38.490 --> 00:42:39.950
How long did that last for?

00:42:39.950 --> 00:42:41.384
About five, six years.

00:42:41.974 --> 00:42:43.916
Okay, and then you went to prison
for 10 years.

00:42:43.916 --> 00:42:44.916
Yeah.

00:42:44.917 --> 00:42:47.547
How was that? I mean you probably
don't want to talk about it.

00:42:47.547 --> 00:42:49.202
-It was pretty neat.
-It was pretty neat?

00:42:49.202 --> 00:42:51.630
Yeah. I mean,
I went to Eglin Air Force Base.

00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:52.980
It's like a Boy Scout camp.

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:56.232
Work out, gym, no fences.

00:42:56.804 --> 00:42:57.770
It was pretty neat.

00:42:57.770 --> 00:42:59.713
I did half of my time there.

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:02.109
When I got out, I got busted.

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:03.500
I say 10 years.

00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:07.171
I got busted for a rifle in my truck
and he give me another five years.

00:43:07.171 --> 00:43:08.941
-That wasn't fun.
-Are you serious?

00:43:08.941 --> 00:43:10.315
-Yeah. That was serious.
-Oh, man.

00:43:10.315 --> 00:43:12.367
Then I did five years in a state prison.

00:43:12.925 --> 00:43:13.978
That wasn't fun.

00:43:14.600 --> 00:43:15.970
That Florida state prisons.

00:43:16.450 --> 00:43:18.018
The federal prisons are way better?

00:43:18.018 --> 00:43:20.036
Oh, man. Come on.

00:43:20.036 --> 00:43:23.013
That Eglin Air Force Base was like a dream.

00:43:23.890 --> 00:43:26.542
I mean, if I would have went
to state prison the very first time,

00:43:26.542 --> 00:43:28.190
I'd have never broke another law.

00:43:28.432 --> 00:43:31.363
-From what you did before to now this.
This is peaceful now, huh?

00:43:31.363 --> 00:43:32.376
-Oh, it's great.

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:36.024
I do tours in
the Big Cypress National Preserve.

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:37.716
I love my job.

00:43:38.457 --> 00:43:40.512
-Steve, what were you saying?
We're screwing you up?

00:43:40.512 --> 00:43:43.612
Screwing me up. I got sh*t I gotta do.
Come on. Get on. Get on.

00:43:44.146 --> 00:43:46.543
All right.
Steve's giving us a little ride here.

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:48.157
I got it. Yeah.

00:43:48.391 --> 00:43:50.761
-[Harrison] Can't be mad at that.
-[Peter] Thank you, Steve.

00:43:50.761 --> 00:43:53.430
-Appreciate it.
-Those people there loved it.

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:55.730
[Harrison] Where were they from?

00:43:56.959 --> 00:43:58.959
Hell, I don't know where they were from.

00:43:59.676 --> 00:44:01.326
Let's see how much they loved it.

00:44:02.725 --> 00:44:03.725
Gave me a 20.

00:44:04.180 --> 00:44:05.790
[Harrison] That's somewhat loved it.

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:08.711
-[Peter] They're paying… 
-That's good. That's nice of them.

00:44:08.711 --> 00:44:11.650
If they give me $5, it's nice of them.
You know what I mean?

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:12.697
-Yeah.
-I don't…

00:44:13.839 --> 00:44:15.619
People work hard for their money.

00:44:15.619 --> 00:44:16.619
[engine starts]

00:44:17.358 --> 00:44:18.644
I can't…

00:44:18.644 --> 00:44:21.646
[Peter] So this is your full-time gig?
This is what you do?

00:44:22.749 --> 00:44:23.949
I don't smuggle no more.

00:44:24.353 --> 00:44:25.353
This is what I do.

00:44:25.353 --> 00:44:26.553
-Less money, less stress.

00:44:27.235 --> 00:44:28.801
A lot less money.

00:44:30.062 --> 00:44:32.211
-What's it like
when you make that much money?

00:44:32.211 --> 00:44:33.888
What does it do to your head?

00:44:33.888 --> 00:44:35.588
Makes you think you're invincible.

00:44:36.841 --> 00:44:38.541
Makes you think you're invincible.

00:44:39.132 --> 00:44:40.432
You can buy everybody off.

00:44:41.304 --> 00:44:43.604
When you got that much money,
everybody thinks…

00:44:44.141 --> 00:44:45.431
you're the man!

00:44:45.648 --> 00:44:47.198
They all want to be around you.

00:44:47.682 --> 00:44:49.331
Because you got so much money.

00:44:49.573 --> 00:44:50.573
Right.

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:54.362
[Steve] Which is all right? I understand.

00:44:54.859 --> 00:44:56.627
You know, and I treated people good.

00:44:57.078 --> 00:45:00.532
I found 10 grand in my truck
one day behind the seat.

00:45:00.993 --> 00:45:02.293
It was close to Christmas.

00:45:02.690 --> 00:45:04.290
I went out and give it all away.

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:06.975
Somebody asked me, said,
you still got my rifle?

00:45:06.975 --> 00:45:10.415
I said, I don't know. Let me check
and see if I got a .22 Magnum.

00:45:10.415 --> 00:45:11.987
Let me see it might be behind the seat.

00:45:11.987 --> 00:45:14.610
When I flipped the seat up,
there was a rolled up paper sack.

00:45:14.610 --> 00:45:17.560
I said, what the hell was that?
I opened up. It was $10,000.

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:20.080
That and an ounce of cocaine.

00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:23.610
Good cocaine from Miami, right off the key.

00:45:24.099 --> 00:45:27.090
So, I went and got me
a bottle of Crown Royal.

00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:28.193
Went out to Copeland.

00:45:28.193 --> 00:45:29.910
Used to be the Black section and stuff.

00:45:29.910 --> 00:45:33.979
We'd build a big fire and threw that
ounce of cocaine on the tailgate

00:45:33.979 --> 00:45:35.612
with a bottle of Crown.

00:45:35.612 --> 00:45:39.025
And I started handing out
that 10 grand to everybody.

00:45:39.553 --> 00:45:40.750
We had a good time.

00:45:40.750 --> 00:45:42.640
They had a good Christmas, them folks.

00:45:45.151 --> 00:45:46.570
You don't do that stuff anymore?

00:45:46.570 --> 00:45:48.410
No sir. I don't even drink.

00:45:48.410 --> 00:45:50.336
I don't smoke. I don't drink.

00:45:50.336 --> 00:45:54.163
I got a 20-year-old daughter
I raised from a baby.

00:45:54.652 --> 00:45:58.502
I didn't have no girlfriends or nothing
because I wanted to raise my daughter.

00:45:58.502 --> 00:45:59.570
Oh, that's cool.

00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:01.270
So, you've been clean 20 years?

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:03.051
-Oh, longer than that.
-Oh, nice.

00:46:03.051 --> 00:46:04.901
I went to work for the park first.

00:46:05.312 --> 00:46:06.312
-Right.

00:46:06.312 --> 00:46:07.627
You know, they say…

00:46:08.223 --> 00:46:09.688
-Can I sit up on this?
-Yeah.

00:46:11.785 --> 00:46:15.438
As long as you are clean for 10 years,

00:46:17.812 --> 00:46:20.710
and you don't have no violent crimes,
the park will hire you.

00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:22.410
A lot of felons work for the park.

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:24.367
-Okay, good.
-So, I was clean.

00:46:24.367 --> 00:46:25.910
I was over 10 years.

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:27.310
I've got my rights restored.

00:46:27.943 --> 00:46:29.243
I can vote and everything.

00:46:30.227 --> 00:46:32.404
So, this whole road right here,

00:46:32.404 --> 00:46:34.390
the reason it's built up like this,

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:38.576
is because it goes into
an exploration oil well.

00:46:38.970 --> 00:46:41.898
The Big Cypress has oil underneath of it.

00:46:41.898 --> 00:46:44.688
There's two or three
active oil wells right now,

00:46:45.134 --> 00:46:46.796
down towards Dade County.

00:46:47.518 --> 00:46:48.802
It runs all the way up.

00:46:49.238 --> 00:46:50.808
It's just south of Immokalee.

00:46:50.808 --> 00:46:52.171
The oil line does.

00:46:52.663 --> 00:46:54.441
It's called Sunnyland Oil Field.

00:46:54.441 --> 00:46:56.041
Runs all the way to Dade County.

00:46:56.244 --> 00:46:56.848
-Okay.

00:46:56.848 --> 00:46:59.449
It's got it piped
across the prairies down there.

00:46:59.744 --> 00:47:01.415
I don't know where the pipeline goes.

00:47:02.142 --> 00:47:03.866
It's just crude oil,

00:47:04.332 --> 00:47:08.010
making tires and roads and stuff like that.

00:47:09.406 --> 00:47:11.789
Hey, I'd take y'all
for a trip tonight and ride you,

00:47:11.790 --> 00:47:13.540
but I've got to go get my rear end.

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:15.770
It's my recovery buggy.

00:47:16.148 --> 00:47:18.448
I do all the recovery work
for the Big Cypress.

00:47:18.858 --> 00:47:21.165
If somebody comes
and gets stuck or something,

00:47:21.462 --> 00:47:22.767
I've got to go get them.

00:47:22.767 --> 00:47:25.588
And they count on me, you know.

00:47:25.588 --> 00:47:26.421
And I don't…

00:47:26.422 --> 00:47:27.885
That's my recovery buggy.

00:47:28.465 --> 00:47:30.536
It's a Land Rover rear end.

00:47:30.894 --> 00:47:34.080
Which Land Rovers are made
for farming and ranching

00:47:34.081 --> 00:47:36.056
and stuff like that.

00:47:36.498 --> 00:47:37.498
So…

00:47:37.855 --> 00:47:40.050
[Peter] Tight community
out here in the Everglades?

00:47:40.056 --> 00:47:41.296
-Yeah. Very tight.

00:47:41.690 --> 00:47:43.470
Chokoloskee is the main place.

00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:44.970
-Okay.
-That's where it happens.

00:47:44.970 --> 00:47:47.930
We went to that shop,
that beautiful shop there.

00:47:47.931 --> 00:47:49.729
-Smallwood.
-You went to the Smallwood store?

00:47:50.059 --> 00:47:52.519
-A couple years ago.
-That's my cousin's place.

00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:54.470
I made a video. She was in it.

00:47:54.470 --> 00:47:55.470
Lynn.

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:57.197
Lynn was in the video a couple years ago.

00:47:57.197 --> 00:47:58.909
That's my cousin.
We got the same granddad.

00:47:58.909 --> 00:48:01.895
Do you worry at all about
this culture going away?

00:48:01.895 --> 00:48:03.410
-It's going away.
-It's going away.

00:48:03.410 --> 00:48:05.199
Ain't just worried about it.
It's going away.

00:48:05.199 --> 00:48:06.350
What do you mean?

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:07.350
It's…

00:48:07.594 --> 00:48:09.094
Everything's tightening it up.

00:48:09.489 --> 00:48:11.190
Can't do this. Can't do that.

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:12.701
Can't go here. Can't go there.

00:48:12.701 --> 00:48:13.773
It's all changing.

00:48:15.190 --> 00:48:17.870
You can jump off and clock
this gate for me if you would.

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:18.870
[Harrison] Yep.

00:48:20.379 --> 00:48:22.430
Be careful looking down
at the bottom of that gate.

00:48:22.430 --> 00:48:24.230
We've had a big moccasin down there.

00:48:24.601 --> 00:48:25.390
I'll wait on you.

00:48:25.937 --> 00:48:27.026
Be careful.

00:48:27.026 --> 00:48:29.235
I'd spend some time with you, brother, but…

00:48:29.235 --> 00:48:31.250
-I appreciate… No, this is very cool.

00:48:31.250 --> 00:48:34.190
If he's here tomorrow or something,
we could hang out at the house.

00:48:34.190 --> 00:48:35.538
-I appreciate it.

00:48:35.538 --> 00:48:38.001
So, Steve, culture's going away.

00:48:38.001 --> 00:48:39.880
-What about…
-Culture's going away, brother.

00:48:39.880 --> 00:48:41.770
-Always has.
-Are the kids moving out?

00:48:41.771 --> 00:48:43.870
Kids don't have… Kids are moving out.

00:48:43.870 --> 00:48:45.270
Ain't nothing for them here.

00:48:45.719 --> 00:48:46.719
Um…

00:48:47.135 --> 00:48:48.470
Everything's changing.

00:48:49.390 --> 00:48:50.470
Everything's changing.

00:48:50.470 --> 00:48:51.725
There ain't no more old school.

00:48:51.725 --> 00:48:53.149
Ain't no more old ways.

00:48:54.882 --> 00:48:55.956
Well, Steve, thank you.

00:48:55.956 --> 00:48:56.906
Oh, you're welcome.

00:48:57.334 --> 00:49:00.092
For those that want to do this
and get deep in there,

00:49:00.758 --> 00:49:03.310
and get the great stories
from you, the local,

00:49:03.594 --> 00:49:04.642
how do they find you?

00:49:04.642 --> 00:49:07.713
You got to go to Captain Steve's
Swamp Buggy Adventures.

00:49:08.265 --> 00:49:09.822
Call my home number.

00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:12.950
Leave a message.
If I'm not there, I'll call you back.

00:49:13.410 --> 00:49:16.775
But listen, it's the awesomest thing
you've ever done.

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:19.080
If you want to spend a few hours…

00:49:19.080 --> 00:49:21.051
Hey, I do nighttime rides.

00:49:21.051 --> 00:49:22.687
I do snake hunting trips.

00:49:23.100 --> 00:49:26.330
We'll start a campfire and cook your food.

00:49:26.980 --> 00:49:28.350
Bring all the kids.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:29.530
Bring some hot dogs.

00:49:29.530 --> 00:49:30.768
We'll do all that.

00:49:30.768 --> 00:49:32.880
-I appreciate it. Thank you.
-Thank you, Steve.

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:34.070
Well, thanks, brother.

00:49:34.070 --> 00:49:35.170
Thank y'all very much.

00:49:35.170 --> 00:49:36.970
-It's so cool.
-Pleasure to meet you.

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:41.189
My son's truck,
he got killed four years ago.

00:49:41.189 --> 00:49:42.170
I'm sorry, man.

00:49:42.700 --> 00:49:43.950
Like, it killed me, dude.

00:49:44.782 --> 00:49:47.311
You ever watch Josh Gates on

00:49:47.312 --> 00:49:49.640
Destination Unknown, Destination X?

00:49:50.230 --> 00:49:53.574
He had a room with 10 people that had died.

00:49:54.590 --> 00:49:57.890
One of them was dead
for 45 minutes in ice water.

00:49:59.610 --> 00:50:01.339
Every one of them

00:50:02.086 --> 00:50:03.605
said they didn't want to come back.

00:50:04.687 --> 00:50:07.681
-Wow.
-Every one of them said the same thing.

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:09.710
"We didn't want to come back.

00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:11.978
We died.

00:50:12.490 --> 00:50:13.610
We didn't want to come back.

00:50:13.611 --> 00:50:15.411
It was so nice

00:50:16.371 --> 00:50:18.135
and so beautiful

00:50:18.814 --> 00:50:20.599
that we did not want to come back,

00:50:20.914 --> 00:50:23.621
that somebody told us
that our time wasn't up,

00:50:23.622 --> 00:50:24.872
that we had to come home."

00:50:25.193 --> 00:50:28.900
So that makes you feel good,
you know, if you lost somebody like me.

00:50:29.369 --> 00:50:31.590
Jesus… God,

00:50:32.970 --> 00:50:34.385
is building an army.

00:50:34.692 --> 00:50:37.301
You know the world's
going to hell in a handbasket.

00:50:37.301 --> 00:50:38.610
You know, you can see it.

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:40.460
I'm not a throwing Christian. I'm not.

00:50:40.763 --> 00:50:42.230
I believe. I'm a believer.

00:50:42.744 --> 00:50:45.081
But I believe that God is building an army

00:50:45.463 --> 00:50:49.058
because I have seen so many

00:50:50.129 --> 00:50:53.209
people my son's age die.

00:50:53.766 --> 00:50:55.854
He just lost his best friend, same age.

00:50:56.073 --> 00:50:58.914
-From what, if you don't mind me asking?
-Just from all different stuff.

00:50:58.914 --> 00:51:01.336
My son was killed on a motorcycle.
The other one was killed…

00:51:01.336 --> 00:51:02.336
He had um,

00:51:03.377 --> 00:51:05.857
uh, gas, gastroentero problems.

00:51:06.197 --> 00:51:08.246
But I'm just saying, God's building

00:51:08.991 --> 00:51:09.991
an army.

00:51:09.991 --> 00:51:13.847
He's building an army
with these young strong people, you know,

00:51:14.323 --> 00:51:16.933
because it's gonna come down to it one day.

00:51:17.430 --> 00:51:18.924
It's got to.

00:51:18.924 --> 00:51:21.384
This world can't keep going
like it's going.

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:26.011
I mean, you got to admit,
things ain't looking good.

00:51:26.820 --> 00:51:28.057
Positive note.

00:51:28.817 --> 00:51:29.718
Be good.

00:51:29.718 --> 00:51:31.751
Be a good person. Treat people right.

00:51:33.109 --> 00:51:34.187
Treat people

00:51:35.035 --> 00:51:36.960
the way you would like to be treated.

00:51:36.961 --> 00:51:38.779
I don't care if you're on drugs.

00:51:38.779 --> 00:51:40.981
I don't care if you're an alcoholic.

00:51:41.516 --> 00:51:43.099
You can get off it all.

00:51:43.510 --> 00:51:45.220
I've been on it all. I've done it all.

00:51:45.221 --> 00:51:46.753
I've been a drug addict.

00:51:46.754 --> 00:51:49.206
I've sold stuff

00:51:49.207 --> 00:51:51.026
just to buy pain pills.

00:51:52.530 --> 00:51:55.033
Listen, you can do good.

00:51:55.330 --> 00:51:56.948
You can, you can do good.

00:51:57.300 --> 00:51:59.814
And money ain't everything.

00:51:59.814 --> 00:52:00.858
It ain't about money.

00:52:00.858 --> 00:52:02.815
It's about enjoying yourself,

00:52:03.375 --> 00:52:04.689
being good to your family.

00:52:04.689 --> 00:52:06.443
And I'm being from the heart about that.

00:52:06.450 --> 00:52:07.850
That's the way I look at it,

00:52:08.522 --> 00:52:09.455
you know, and

00:52:10.105 --> 00:52:11.760
save your things.

00:52:12.060 --> 00:52:13.581
Like you walk into my living room,

00:52:13.582 --> 00:52:15.382
I got old things in there, you know,

00:52:15.723 --> 00:52:19.477
stuff from my kids
and pictures all over the walls

00:52:19.477 --> 00:52:20.996
and old

00:52:21.953 --> 00:52:23.924
posted signs and stuff.

00:52:23.924 --> 00:52:26.969
You know, it's not going to be here
in hundreds of years.

00:52:26.969 --> 00:52:28.790
So… yeah.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:31.014
-[Harrison] But the videos will.
-The videos will.

00:52:31.014 --> 00:52:32.689
-Steve, good stuff.
-All right. Thank you.

00:52:32.689 --> 00:52:33.710
I'll be talking to you.

00:52:33.710 --> 00:52:34.608
-All right, Steve.

00:52:34.608 --> 00:52:36.570
-I hope, I hope you do good
with that video.

00:52:36.570 --> 00:52:37.521
I really do.

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:47.990
[Peter] Well, Harrison, thank you, man.

00:52:47.990 --> 00:52:49.030
Very cool day.

00:52:49.030 --> 00:52:50.391
Awesome. I'm glad you enjoyed it.

00:52:50.391 --> 00:52:52.330
-Glad you enjoyed it.
-Awesome. Loved it.

00:52:52.330 --> 00:52:54.017
Yeah, we met a lot of different people.

00:52:54.017 --> 00:52:56.469
We unpacked all the different layers.

00:52:56.469 --> 00:52:58.210
That was the coolest part about it.

00:52:58.210 --> 00:53:01.616
And so, for people wanting this experience,

00:53:01.617 --> 00:53:02.910
Harrison's your man.

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:04.195
Harrison can get you in.

00:53:04.195 --> 00:53:06.195
We did another video a couple years ago.

00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:09.130
Onto the water in his boat.

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:10.202
Yeah.

00:53:10.202 --> 00:53:13.560
But more these days, you're on land.

00:53:13.561 --> 00:53:16.230
Yes, yes. And I'm happy
to refer to any boat tours.

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:19.402
I have a couple friends
who are captains and can do that.

00:53:19.675 --> 00:53:22.546
But as far as knowing
the different spots of the Everglades,

00:53:22.546 --> 00:53:24.530
the history, connecting the dots,

00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:27.004
meeting all the different personalities
that are out here.

00:53:27.004 --> 00:53:28.553
And as far as getting
a lay of the land

00:53:28.553 --> 00:53:30.891
and getting a true immersion
into the Everglades,

00:53:30.891 --> 00:53:32.317
that's not just the touristy stuff.

00:53:32.317 --> 00:53:33.150
-Yeah.

00:53:33.150 --> 00:53:35.077
That's what I really specialize in,
you know,

00:53:35.077 --> 00:53:37.220
personalized to what you're trying to…

00:53:37.220 --> 00:53:39.729
what you're trying to see
and what you're trying to accomplish.

00:53:39.729 --> 00:53:43.430
I'm going to leave your, you have a link
of some sort, I'll leave that down below.

00:53:43.430 --> 00:53:45.256
Website, Instagram, YouTube,

00:53:45.256 --> 00:53:48.040
all up to a bunch of adventures
and just how to, you know,

00:53:48.040 --> 00:53:50.237
inspire wilderness
and get to the different corners.

00:53:50.237 --> 00:53:53.170
-Get in this. The Florida
you don't know for the most part.

00:53:53.170 --> 00:53:57.335
I guarantee 99% of the people
haven't been to this part of the state.

00:53:57.574 --> 00:54:00.474
All right, guys. Thanks for coming
on that journey.

00:54:00.475 --> 00:54:01.475
Until the next one.

00:54:01.475 --> 00:54:02.475
[country music]

